Here is the feedback we got for generated code for data_preprocess.py in the conversation:

<FEEDBACK>- Compute percent.mt instead of defaulting to 0: derive it from cleaned symbols (symbol_clean startswith "MT-") using raw counts before any filtering, e.g., via scanpy.pp.calculate_qc_metrics with adata.var['mt'] set. This makes QC and nuisance-covariate handling meaningful, improving clustering quality.
- Make QC thresholds batch-aware: compute nCount_RNA/nFeature_RNA MAD filters within each domain/dataset to avoid disproportionately removing cells from specific batches, which harms batch mixing and ARI.
- Preserve prior/marker genes through gene QC: relax the “expressed in ≥3 cells” rule for genes with high prior weights or in marker sets so rare but informative markers are not discarded.
- Run HVG on counts and explicitly union per batch: for flavor="seurat_v3", pass layer="counts" (or run before normalization), and regardless of environment support, compute per-batch top-n and take the union, then add prior-preserve genes and cap at 6000. The current path with a single seurat_v3 call can omit batch-specific HVGs and reduce ARI.
- Prevent data leakage in scaling/regression: split first, fit scaling parameters (and any regression, if used) on train only, then apply to val/test. Current code scales and regresses on the full dataset before splitting, which can blur batch/domain structure and harm generalization.
- Make regress_out optional and default off: you already penalize nuisance in the model (nCount_RNA, percent.mt) and do MMD; double-correcting in preprocessing can remove biological signal. Keep a corrected layer for diagnostics but leave X as scaled log1p by default.
- Ensure domain key robustness: if obs['domain'] is missing, fall back to the most informative available field (dataset/sample/batch/library/experiment) rather than a uniform "all", to keep batch-aware HVG and stratification effective.
- Strengthen stratified split logic: guarantee at least one val and one test cell per domain when domain size permits; for very small domains allocate all to train to avoid unreliable validation/test signals. Log per-domain split counts.
- Handle duplicate gene symbols: collapse duplicates (sum counts) prior to QC/HVG using symbol_clean, keeping a map for reversibility. Duplicates otherwise break prior alignment and dilute signal, reducing ARI.
- Fix prior-preserve-by-set detection: do not fall back to “all vocab” when gene_index is absent; require explicit mapping between set_incidence columns and gene_vocab. Over-including genes here breaches the 6000 cap with low-value genes and hurts downstream clustering.
- Save all required artifacts for reproducibility and downstream: export a combined anndata_processed.h5ad (X=scaled log1p), gene_list.txt (ordered var), cell_ids_{train|val|test}.txt, and index_{train|val|test}.npy; copy priors into preprocess output dir.
- Improve QC transparency and stability: log how many cells/genes were removed for each QC criterion (mt, nCount, nFeature, banned, vocab), and assert a minimum retained fraction; abort early if aggressive filters decimate particular domains.
- Keep housekeeping/ribosomal genes deprioritized but not forcibly removed if they are prior-preserve/marker genes; this maintains interpretability without losing key markers.
- Maintain covariates integrity post-subsetting: recompute and persist percent.mt, nCount_RNA, nFeature_RNA after filtering so downstream nuisance penalties use accurate values.
- Enforce consistent gene order across splits and export it: the model relies on exact alignment; add a sanity check that var_names are identical and in the same order in all split files.
- Seed-sensitive steps: set and record seeds for any stochastic preprocessing (e.g., splitting), and write them to metadata; you do this partially—also include the chosen batch_key, number of HVGs pre/post prior-preserve, and count of prior-preserve genes retained.
- Memory safety for regression: warn or switch off regression when matrices would densify beyond safe limits; chunked regression or skipping it prevents OOMs that could force fallback settings and degrade results.</FEEDBACK>

