<FEEDBACK>- Tune the neighborhood graph jointly with clustering: grid-search n_neighbors (e.g., 10, 20, 30, 50), distance metric (cosine vs euclidean), and Leiden resolution; pick the combo maximizing train+val silhouette. This directly optimizes cluster separability and typically boosts ARI.
- Normalize embeddings for cosine: L2-normalize rows of the latent before building a cosine kNN; cosine on non-normalized vectors can distort neighborhoods and hurt ARI.
- Stability/consensus selection: for each (n_neighbors, metric, resolution), run Leiden across multiple random seeds; select the partition with highest mean silhouette and highest stability (e.g., average pairwise ARI/VI across seeds). More stable partitions tend to align better with biology, improving ARI.
- Train/val-only clustering, test assignment: build the kNN and run Leiden on train+val only (for model selection); then assign test cells by nearest-centroid or kNN label transfer from train+val. This avoids test-driven graph effects that can degrade separability, often increasing overall ARI.
- Post-hoc cleanup to raise purity: (a) reassign low-confidence cells (per-cell silhouette below a threshold) to their nearest alternative cluster; (b) merge micro-clusters below a min size into the nearest large cluster; (c) recursively split large, heterogeneous clusters (low intra-cluster silhouette) with a local Leiden sweep. These steps raise cluster purity and improve ARI.
- Compare clustering families and pick by silhouette: evaluate k-means (with k from DEC k_opt if available in logs, plus ±2), Gaussian Mixture (BIC-selected k), Louvain, and HDBSCAN; choose the best train+val silhouette. Different data manifolds favor different algorithms; selecting the best unsupervised option can lift ARI.
- Use DEC signal: if train_log.json exposes k_opt, include it in the candidate k for k-means or constrain Leiden’s resolution to produce k near k_opt; DEC’s k is often close to the ground-truth cardinality, improving ARI when honored downstream.
- Per-domain standardization for graph building: fit the scaler on train+val but also z-score within domain before kNN to reduce residual batch effects that blur cluster boundaries; this typically sharpens clusters and improves ARI.
- Graph-based label smoothing: after initial assignments, perform one round of kNN majority-vote refinement (e.g., reassign if >60% of neighbors belong to a different cluster). This reduces boundary noise and increases ARI.
- Evaluation-driven selection (if permissible): in addition to silhouette, compute ARI on val only for each candidate and pick the clustering that maximizes val ARI; then apply to all cells. This directly optimizes the target metric.</FEEDBACK>

