Here is the feedback we got for generated code for data_preprocess.py in the conversation:

<FEEDBACK>- Enforce a hard gene cap. The current selection uses cap = max(GENE_CAP, prior_preserve.sum()), which led to ~26k genes and bloated S/A/weights. Change this to strictly cap at GENE_CAP and prioritize inclusion: keep all marker genes first, then high-weight prior genes (e.g., weight ≥ 0.8), both still requiring minimal expression, and fill the remaining slots with HVGs ranked by batch-robust dispersion. This will reduce dimensionality, stabilize training, and improve clustering quality.

- Do not bypass expression filtering for prior-set genes. The current gene_keep_mask keeps any gene in priors regardless of being expressed (≥3 cells). Change ((mask_keep_vocab & mask_expr) | prior_preserve_any) to:
  - Always require mask_expr for all genes, except a small top tier of marker/high-weight genes.
  - Further down-rank or exclude genes flagged as ubiquitous (e.g., “in >20% of sets” from priors) even if in a set, unless they are strong markers.
  This removes thousands of uninformative zeros that dilute the signal.

- Make prior-preserve prioritization explicit and deterministic. Within prior-preserve candidates, rank by:
  1) is_marker (True first),
  2) gene_prior_weight (descending),
  3) set-degree (ascending; fewer sets preferred),
  4) batch-robust dispersion on train (descending).
  Then take as many as fit under the hard cap before adding non-prior HVGs.

- Fix the batch-aware HVG fallback. compute_batch_robust_hvg currently accumulates per-batch vectors of size ad_b.n_vars into arrays sized adata.n_vars, which can silently misalign or crash. Change it to:
  - Run HVG per batch on ad_b; map the resulting per-gene scores back to the global var index with a boolean mask (ad_b.var_names alignment).
  - Aggregate scores across batches on the global index (e.g., mean z-dispersion), then select top n_top_genes globally.
  Also ensure the fallback uses the same “seurat_v3” flavor or a robust variance residual to match the intended behavior.

- Tighten QC to remove nuisance-driven cells/genes that harm clustering:
  - Add optional doublet removal (e.g., Scrublet) post-QC and pre-split; gate doublets by score threshold with an override to keep high-confidence singlets.
  - Increase the minimal expression threshold for HVG consideration to ≥5 cells on the train split (retain prior-preserved markers regardless).
  - Add a train-only nuisance filter: drop genes with high absolute Pearson correlation to percent.mt or nCount_RNA (e.g., >0.3) unless they are prioritized by prior weight/marker status.

- Improve scaling stability:
  - Clip Z-scores more aggressively (|z| ≤ 5) to reduce the influence of outliers that degrade downstream batch alignment and clustering.
  - Log in metadata the train-only mu/sd used for scaling and the final selected gene list with their ranks to ensure exact reproducibility across reruns.

- Ensure prior artifacts match the selected gene space for downstream consumption:
  - In subset_and_save_priors, drop zero-coverage sets after column subsetting (rows with nnz == 0), reindex set_meta accordingly, and save a companion set_meta_reindexed.csv in PREPROCESS_DIR. This keeps the pathway dimension compact and avoids dead rows in the set bottleneck used by downstream models.
  - Save a set_mask.npy marking non-empty sets so training can optionally use it (even if current training code doesn’t, this enables low-effort adoption and avoids silent mismatches).

- Make the split more robust for tiny domains. If a domain has very few cells, force at least 1 val and 1 test cell only when n_d ≥ 20; otherwise keep them all in train to avoid unstable HVG and scaling stats from singletons.

- Persist complete logs. Write preprocessing decisions (n genes after each filter, how many prior-preserve genes admitted vs dropped by cap, number of zeroed sets removed) to PREPROCESS_METADATA and flush periodically. This helps diagnose regressions in ARI tied to preprocessing.</FEEDBACK>


