<LM_OUTPUT> {"role": "data_science", "feedback": "Key fixes to feature selection, prior alignment, and normalization to match the spec and avoid leakage/oversized gene spaces:\n\n1) Enforce hard feature cap (<=6000) with principled ranking, don’t let prior-preserve blow past the cap.\n- Problem: cap = max(GENE_CAP, prior_preserve.sum()) yields 26k genes (see preprocess metadata), which violates the 6k cap and hurts clustering.\n- Fix: rank prior-preserve genes by (prior weight desc, train dispersion desc), keep up to GENE_CAP, then fill any remaining slots with top-dispersion HVGs not yet included.\n\nReplace the selection block with:\n\n# compute weights/dispersion on TRAIN only\nvar_syms = adata.var[\"symbol_clean\"].astype(str).str.upper().str.strip().values\nw = np.array([float(symbol_to_weight.get(s, 0.0)) for s in var_syms], dtype=float)\nX_log_train = ad_train.layers[\"log1p\"].A if sparse.issparse(ad_train.layers[\"log1p\"]) else np.asarray(ad_train.layers[\"log1p\"])\ntrain_disp = np.var(X_log_train, axis=0)\n\n# prior-preserve mask (recomputed after subsetting to ensure alignment)\npp_weight = (w >= 0.5)\npp_set = np.array([s in priors[\"symbols_in_sets\"] for s in var_syms], dtype=bool)\nprior_preserve = pp_weight | pp_set\n\n# enforce cap strictly\ncap = GENE_CAP\nselected = np.zeros(adata.n_vars, dtype=bool)\npp_idx = np.where(prior_preserve)[0]\nif len(pp_idx) > 0:\n    # lexsort sorts by last key first; use (-w, -disp) for descending\n    order_pp = pp_idx[np.lexsort((-train_disp[pp_idx], -w[pp_idx]))]\n    keep_pp = order_pp[:min(len(order_pp), cap)]\n    selected[keep_pp] = True\n\nif selected.sum() < cap:\n    # add HVGs by dispersion among remaining genes\n    remaining = np.where(~selected)[0]\n    add_k = min(cap - int(selected.sum()), len(remaining))\n    if add_k > 0:\n        add_idx = remaining[np.argsort(-train_disp[remaining])[:add_k]]\n        selected[add_idx] = True\n\nadata = adata[:, selected].copy()\n\n2) Apply gene QC consistently and leakage-averse (train-only expression), and don’t allow prior-preserve to bypass basic QC.\n- Problem: ((keep_flag & expr) | prior_preserve) allows unexpressed prior genes; also expression is computed on all cells (leaks test into filtering).\n- Fix:\n\n# TRAIN-only expression filter (counts before normalization)\ntrain_idx = np.where(adata.obs[\"split\"].values == \"train\")[0]\nexpr_train = compute_nonzero_per_gene(adata.X[train_idx])\nmask_expr = expr_train >= 3\n\n# require keep_flag, not banned, and expression>=3\nallowed_symbols = set(df_vocab.loc[df_vocab[\"keep_flag\"].astype(bool), \"symbol_clean\"].str.upper().str.strip().values)\nvar_syms_upper = adata.var[\"symbol_clean\"].astype(str).str.upper().str.strip()\nmask_keep_vocab = np.array([s in allowed_symbols for s in var_syms_upper.values], dtype=bool)\nmask_not_banned = ~np.array([s in priors[\"banned_symbols\"] for s in var_syms_upper.values], dtype=bool)\n\ngene_keep_mask = mask_keep_vocab & mask_not_banned & mask_expr\nadata = adata[:, gene_keep_mask].copy()\n\n3) Use correct input for HVG with Seurat v3: raw counts layer, not log1p.\n- Problem: sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(..., flavor=\"seurat_v3\", layer=\"log1p\") is suboptimal; Seurat v3 expects counts.\n- Fix:\n\nsc.pp.highly_variable_genes(\n    ad_train,\n    flavor=\"seurat_v3\",\n    n_top_genes=min(N_TOP_HVG, ad_train.n_vars),\n    batch_key=batch_key if batch_key in ad_train.obs.columns else None,\n    layer=\"counts\",\n)\n\nIf fallback is triggered, also use layer=\"counts\" in compute_batch_robust_hvg.\n\n4) Minor alignment/consistency guards:\n- When building prior-preserve masks and ranking, recompute on the post-QC variable set (var_syms from current adata) to ensure symbol alignment with S/A mapping.\n- After final selection, re-run subset_and_save_priors with the final var order (already done) and assert that S_sub.shape[1] == adata.n_vars and A_sub.shape == (adata.n_vars, adata.n_vars).\n\n5) Metadata sanity:\n- Update preprocess_metadata hvg.cap to reflect the true enforced cap (6000), and include n_genes_final = adata.n_vars to verify adherence.\n\nNet effect: you’ll reduce the feature space to ~6k high-value genes (prior-weighted and data-driven), eliminate leakage from test in gene QC, and use HVG on appropriate inputs, which should improve stability and clustering quality (ARI) while keeping priors correctly aligned to the model input."} </LM_OUTPUT>

