Here is the feedback we got for generated code for data_preprocess.py in the conversation:

<FEEDBACK>- Enforce a strict 6000-gene cap after prior-preserve with a principled ranking, then fill with top train-dispersion HVGs; do not let prior-preserve exceed the cap. Rank prior genes by (prior weight desc, train dispersion desc) so informative small-set/marker genes are kept first.
- Make gene QC leakage-averse and stricter: compute expression≥3 cells on TRAIN only and require it for all genes (including prior-preserve). Optionally require expression in at least two batches to avoid batch-specific zeros.
- Use Seurat v3 HVG on raw counts (layer="counts") with batch_key on TRAIN only. If falling back to custom HVG, derive dispersion on counts and aggregate per-batch z-scores; don’t use log1p as the primary input for HVG.
- After subsetting genes, re-normalize priors: row-normalize S_sub so each set still sums to 1 over remaining genes; re-normalize A_sub rows and re-symmetrize; drop sets that end up with size<5 (or >500) and update set_meta (size, weight=1/sqrt(size)) accordingly. This prevents distorted set activities and graph penalties.
- Fix default prior weights when aligning: missing symbols should default to a neutral/high weight (e.g., 1.0), not 0.0, to avoid unintentionally down-weighting large swaths of genes in reconstruction and loss weighting.
- Preserve prior flags in var and metadata: add var columns prior_preserve, prior_weight, in_any_set to audit selection; log counts kept/dropped per reason (banned, not in vocab, low expression, cap-pruned).
- Tighten confound control without leakage: keep DO_REGRESS=False for X, but if you store a regressed layer, fit the regression on TRAIN only and apply the learned params to val/test; never refit on all cells. Also compute scaling params strictly on TRAIN (already done) and save them with metadata.
- Improve mito/ribo handling: beyond percent.mt QC, exclude mito/ribo genes from the feature set unless explicitly whitelisted by marker sets; at minimum, de-prioritize them when ranking under the cap.
- Strengthen split stratification: enforce a minimum test size per domain (e.g., at least one batch-proportional floor) and avoid dumping remainder to train; this stabilizes downstream alignment and ARI.
- Protect against ComBat leakage in the diagnostic embedding: either disable ComBat in preprocess or fit it on TRAIN only and apply batch adjustments to val/test; otherwise, any clustering/metrics produced here may be optimistic and can bias downstream decisions.
- Validate alignment invariants after selection: assert S_sub.shape[1]==n_genes_final; A_sub.shape==(n_genes_final,n_genes_final); no empty sets remain; set_meta rows match S_sub rows; and all saved priors/layers are float32.
- Cap and clip more robustly: consider clipping scaled z-scores at 5 (not 10) to reduce tail influence, and optionally use robust scaling (median/MAD) on TRAIN when heavy tails are detected.
- Update metadata to reflect the true enforced cap and final gene count; record how many prior-preserve genes survived expression and cap pruning; save a JSON of ranked-by-reason gene lists for reproducibility.
- Optional but impactful: run a light, label-free doublet detection (e.g., Scrublet) pre-QC and remove high-score doublets; this often improves cluster purity and ARI without supervision.</FEEDBACK>

