<LM_OUTPUT> {"role": "model", "feedback": "- Enforce the gene cap in preprocessing to avoid 26k+ input genes (which inflates W_gs, L, and hurts training). In data_preprocess.py, replace cap = max(GENE_CAP, int(prior_preserve.sum())) with cap = GENE_CAP and select prior-preserve by priority: keep all marker genes first, then sort remaining prior-preserve by (gene_prior_weight desc, set_degree asc), then fill up to cap with HVGs. This will also shrink A/L and stabilize Laplacian loss.\n- Delay/guard early stopping so it can see fine-tune and DEC phases. In model_training.py, add min_epochs = finetune_end + 10 and only allow early stop checks once epoch >= min_epochs; additionally reset num_bad = 0 and best_val = val_loss when DEC is first enabled (epoch == finetune_end + 1).\n- Use the precomputed DEC target per batch indices instead of recomputing p_b. You already pass idxs from the dataset; replace p_b with q_target_full[idxs] when q_target_full is available:\n  # inside training loop, DEC block\n  if use_dec and (q_target_full is not None):\n      ...\n      p_b = q_target_full[idxs]  # exact target for this minibatch\n      ldec = F.kl_div(q_b.log(), p_b, reduction=\"batchmean\")\n- Tone down alignment during fine-tune and remove redundancy. Set lam_mmd_eff = 0.0 and lam_dann = 0.0 until epoch > finetune_end; then ramp lam_mmd linearly to 0.2 by epoch 100; keep DANN off (or <=0.01 only after epoch 100). This prevents over-alignment from degrading structure early.\n- Make the validation objective phase-aware to avoid picking the warmup checkpoint by default: for epoch <= warmup_end use recon only; for warmup_end < epoch <= finetune_end use recon + marker + set_bottleneck + decorrelation + orthogonality; only after epoch > finetune_end add a small silhouette bonus (as you already do) and keep MMD out of val loss to reduce minibatch-composition noise.\n- Replace the Laplacian loss computation with an edge-sum formulation to avoid large sparse.mm on very wide gene spaces and to make it scale with graph sparsity:\n  def laplacian_edge_loss(W_eff, A_csr, gene_w):\n      coo = A_csr.tocoo()\n      gij = (gene_w[coo.row] + gene_w[coo.col]) * 0.5\n      dif = W_eff[:, coo.row] - W_eff[:, coo.col]  # [S, |E|]\n      return torch.mean((torch.linalg.norm(dif, dim=0) ** 2) * torch.tensor(coo.data, device=W_eff.device) * torch.tensor(gij, device=W_eff.device))\n  Then use this in place of laplacian_loss; or sample a fixed subset of edges per epoch if memory-bound.\n- Reduce W_gs memory and improve throughput: register S0 and mask as float16 buffers and cast to float32 in ops only if needed; e.g., self.register_buffer(\"S0\", S_tensor.half()); compute W in forward as (self.mask * self.S0.to(x.dtype) * self.scale()). This drops VRAM by ~2x for large S.\n- Logging/QC: persist full per-epoch logs. Ensure training_logs.json contains the logs list (not {}); append every epoch and flush to disk every N epochs to avoid loss if interrupted.\n- Minor schedules: start DEC earlier (e.g., finetune_end=50) once early-stopping is guarded; keep lam_prior anneal as-is, but consider lowering lam_prior_base to 1.0 if you still see val divergence after unfreezing W_gs."} </LM_OUTPUT>

