Here is the feedback we got for generated code for model_training.py in the conversation:

<FEEDBACK>- Prevent warmup checkpoint selection: gate early stopping until after DEC is active. Concretely, require epoch >= finetune_end + 10 before updating best_val/num_bad; when enabling DEC (epoch == finetune_end + 1), reset best_val to current val_loss and num_bad = 0 so later phases are considered.
- Make val objective better aligned with clustering quality: exclude MMD/DANN from val loss (they’re minibatch-composition–noisy), and add a stronger unsupervised clustering signal on val (e.g., silhouette on provisional DEC labels or kmeans on val z) as a small negative term. This reduces the chance that pure-reconstruction warmup wins.
- Use the global DEC target per sample instead of recomputing per-minibatch targets: store q_target_full computed over the full train set and index it with the provided idxs in the dataloader to define p_b = q_target_full[idxs]. This stabilizes the KL term and improves cluster consistency.
- Defer and tone down alignment: set lam_mmd_eff = 0 and lam_dann = 0 during finetune; linearly ramp lam_mmd only after DEC starts (e.g., to 0.2 by epoch 100). Keep DANN off entirely or <=0.01 only after epoch 100. Over-alignment early flattens structure and hurts ARI.
- Apply alignment only to the nuisance subspace: run MMD/DANN and nuisance correlation on z[:, d_marker:] (or on a small learned projection) to protect marker/biological axes. This preserves signal needed for clustering while still reducing batch effects.
- Replace the Laplacian loss sparse.mm with an edge-difference formulation (sum over edges ||W[:,i]−W[:,j]||^2 weighted by A_ij), and optionally sample a fixed number of edges per epoch. This cuts variance/memory and prevents the Laplacian from dominating with large gene spaces.
- Stabilize W_gs memory/throughput: store S0/mask as float16 buffers and cast to x.dtype on use; keep delta/bias in fp32. This reduces VRAM/CPU RAM and speeds the masked matmuls without changing behavior.
- Add per-sample L1 sparsity on set_act (small weight, e.g., 1e-4–1e-3). A sparse pathway activity bottleneck improves separability and interpretability, often boosting ARI.
- Improve DEC robustness: L2-normalize z for DEC consistently; use multi-seed k initialization (already done for kmeans) and keep centers EMA-updated a bit during DEC (small momentum) to reduce target drift.
- Strengthen checkpointing for clustering: persist three candidates (end of warmup, end of finetune, best_val). After training, choose the checkpoint that maximizes val silhouette on latent; use that for final embedding/cluster export. This directly optimizes an unsupervised proxy correlated with ARI.
- Make logs reliable: append per-epoch logs and flush to disk every N epochs; don’t overwrite with {} on empty list. Add val silhouette and (optionally) unsupervised stability metrics to logs for better tuning of λ’s.</FEEDBACK>

