<FEEDBACK>{"role": "data_science", "feedback": "- Stop hard-gating features to priors. Using keep_flag as a hard filter prevented any HVG additions (n_prior_selected == n_genes_after_base_qc == 3839). Change base_keep_mask to: (not banned) AND (expressed>=3) AND (not mito/ribo unless marker). Treat keep_flag as a soft preference, not a requirement. Then allow HVGs outside priors to be selected up to the cap. This will restore data-driven signal and typically boosts ARI.\n- For genes not in priors, preserve them in the final matrix and carry them through prior alignment by inserting zero columns/rows for S/A, and assign small reconstruction weights (e.g., 0.05–0.1) so they don’t dominate the loss while still informing clustering. Export an in_any_set boolean mask aligned to gene_list to let downstream losses handle them differently.\n- Enforce a two-tier feature cap: reserve a fixed fraction (e.g., 40–50%) of slots for batch-stable HVGs not already prior-preserved. Implement stability as present in ≥ceil(B/2) batch-specific top-k lists (computed on TRAIN) and prioritize HVGs with low batch-to-batch variance ratio. This prevents priors from monopolizing the cap and improves unsupervised separability (higher ARI).\n- Improve nuisance filtering robustness: compute partial correlations on TRAIN between log1p expression and {percent.mt, nCount_RNA}, controlling for domain (add domain dummies). Calibrate the drop threshold by an empirical null (e.g., 200 circular-shift permutations per gene) with BH-FDR; drop only if q<0.05 and not strong-prior. This reduces over-pruning true biology while still removing confounded genes.\n- Extend QC covariates and thresholds per domain: (a) compute percent.ribo (from is_ribo) and percent.hb (RBC genes like HBA1/HBA2/HBB/HBD), (b) replace the fixed 20% mt cutoff with per-domain robust limits: upper = clip(median + 3*MAD, 5, 40) for percent.mt and similarly for percent.ribo/percent.hb, and (c) drop cells violating any of these. This reduces chemistry/protocol-induced bias without discarding good cells.\n- Make doublet handling non-destructive by default: always annotate scrublet scores in obs; if removal is desired, fit thresholds per-domain on TRAIN only (e.g., Gaussian mixture or score quantile) and apply those thresholds to VAL/TEST. This avoids domain-specific selection bias and instability that can depress ARI.\n- Adaptive scaling clip: compute per-gene clip on TRAIN as the 99.5th percentile of |z| and bound it to [5, 12]. This preserves informative extremes while preventing saturation or over-truncation.\n- Ensure prior freshness and alignment: don’t hardcode PRIOR_DIR to step_0. Read the latest prior path from a run_config (or detect newest timestamp under runsv3/priors) and validate that gene_vocab/set_incidence/gene_gene_adj all correspond to the same build. Stale priors or mismatched indices can silently degrade cluster quality.\n- Cell-cycle covariates: annotate S and G2M scores via score_genes_cell_cycle (TRAIN-only gene centering), store in obs, and keep these for downstream nuisance penalties (don’t regress here). This helps the model disentangle cell-cycle effects from cell identity, typically improving unsupervised ARI.\n- Post-selection set coverage safeguard: after final gene selection, check reindexed S for any high-priority sets (marker or high-weight) that fell below min size; greedily add the top-variance genes from those sets (not yet selected) up to the cap. This preserves interpretability while keeping strong pathway signal.\n- Save diagnostic masks aligned to gene_list: in_any_set, is_marker, is_prior_preserved, is_batch_stable_hvg, is_nuisance_dropped_before_exemptions. Expose these to training for nuanced loss weighting/monitoring. "}</FEEDBACK>


