Here is the feedback we got for generated code for model_training.py in the conversation:

<FEEDBACK>- Use the reindexed prior artifacts from preprocessing instead of re-aligning to PRIOR_OUTPUT_DIR. Load:
  - set_incidence_reindexed.npz, gene_gene_adj_reindexed.npz, set_meta_reindexed.csv, gene_prior_weights_aligned.csv, and set_mask.npy from step_0. Assert S.shape[1] == ad_train.n_vars, A.shape == (n_genes, n_genes), and set_meta_reindexed rows match S rows after applying set_mask. This removes a second, lossy alignment pass that likely drops valid genes/sets and degrades guidance.
- Normalize and validate priors on load:
  - Ensure S is row-normalized (sum=1); if not, normalize once at load time. Recompute set size after masking zero-coverage rows; set_weights = 1/sqrt(size). Use the pre-normalized A_reindexed; no extra sym/row-norm inside training to avoid divergence with preprocessing.
- Fix marker anchoring target to avoid circularity:
  - Compute per-cell marker proxies with a fixed, non-trainable mapping from genes to sets (e.g., X @ normalize_rows(S0)), or mean over marker gene members per set. Do not use set_act as target.
- Strengthen the training schedule and actually exercise alignment/clustering:
  - Extend epochs to ~150–200; warmup ~30 (W_gs frozen; recon only), structured finetune ~60 (unfreeze W_gs; enable prior/group/lap/marker/nuisance), clustering phase for the remainder (enable DEC with target refresh every 5 epochs).
  - Ramp MMD on z[:, d_marker:] linearly from 0 at finetune start to full by mid-clustering. Currently MMD/DANN are effectively off; this prevents the domain alignment objective from influencing learning.
- Simplify adversarial alignment or make it effective:
  - Either remove DomainAdversary to reduce complexity and focus on MMD, or add a proper cross-entropy domain loss via GRL with a very small weight; avoid keeping a dead module with zero contribution.
- Stabilize Gene→Set weights:
  - Initialize edge scales below 1 (e.g., softplus^{-1}(0.8)) to create gradient signal toward the prior; clamp softplus(delta) to [0, s_max] (e.g., s_max=3) to prevent runaway edges; keep weight decay=0 on gs params but add a tiny L2 on delta (1e-5) to limit drift.
- Improve Laplacian regularization:
  - Use the reindexed A (already normalized) to build edge lists; sample edges proportionally to A_ij (importance sampling) each step and rescale to keep an unbiased estimate; this focuses smoothing on strong gene-gene relations and reduces variance.
- Make batches MMD-friendly:
  - Enforce per-domain balance per minibatch (equal counts per domain where possible), with drop_last=True to avoid tiny residual batches. If memory-bound, use gradient accumulation to maintain a large effective batch for MMD statistics.
- Validation/objective alignment:
  - Include marker anchoring and MMD (with the same ramp factor) in the validation objective so the scheduler/ES reflect alignment quality, not just reconstruction. Keep cosine-silhouette as a separate metric for checkpoint selection.
- Activate and log DEC in the clustering phase:
  - Implement DEC with Student-t soft assignments, KL to target distribution, and target refresh every 5 epochs. Include a “post-DEC” checkpoint candidate in selection. Record k, target refresh iterations, and DEC loss in logs.
- Resolve logging/file clobbering:
  - Write model_training-specific logs (e.g., model_train_log.json) and avoid reusing preprocessing TRAINING_LOGS path. Log every epoch: per-loss components, val_objective, val_silhouette, LR, MMD ramp, and DEC status.
- Checkpoint selection:
  - Evaluate checkpoints from end_warmup, end_finetune, and end_clustering (DEC active) by cosine-silhouette on val using a scaler fit on train+val; keep best state. Record chosen checkpoint and its silhouette.
- Minor robustness:
  - If any splits are empty, guard against zero-length loaders (skip MMD/DEC for that epoch).
  - When computing set activities to export, use the fixed set activations from the forward pass but also provide a “prior-only” activity (X @ normalize_rows(S0)) for interpretability QC.</FEEDBACK>

